<<529>> Give me a short answer exercise for a 3rd grade student learning computer science. <<Prompt>>
<<529>> Why is it important to save work often? Give two reasons.  

\paragraph{Think With Me}
Imagine you are working on a document and you haven't saved it for a while. Suddenly, the electricity goes out or your computer crashes. What would happen to your work? 

\paragraph{Bonus Activity}
If you have access to a computer, open a file and make some changes. Then, simulate a crash or loss of power by closing the file without saving. What happens to your work? Now, open the file again. Is your work still there? Did you lose any changes? Try this with different types of files to see the results. Write down what happens each time.  

\paragraph{Bonus Question}
How can you recover your work if your computer crashes before you can save it? Look for ways to recover unsaved work on your computer or ask an adult for help. Share your findings with the class. Write them down if you can't share verbally.  

\paragraph{Extra Challenge}  
Make a list of times when your computer might crash or you might lose your work. Write down ways to prevent these problems from happening. Present your list to the class. If you can't present verbally, write it down.  

\paragraph{Extra Challenge}  
Why is it important to save work often when using a computer? Describe a situation where saving work often would be helpful. Explain why it's helpful to save work often. Share your ideas with the class or write them down if you can't share verbally.  

\paragraph{Think With Me}
Have you ever lost work because your computer crashed or you forgot to save? Describe what happened and how you felt. Share your experience with the class or write it down if you can't share verbally. What did you do after losing your work? Did you have a backup or did you recreate the work? Share your story.  

\paragraph{Think With Me}
Think about a time when you were working on a document or file and your computer crashed or you lost power before you could save it. What did you do? Did you have a backup or did you recreate the work? Share your experience with the class. If you can't share verbally, write it down. What would you do differently next time? Share your thoughts.  

